Fair enough. You asked for the math, so let me show it explicitly and explain exactly where the scaling argument breaks down.

What a Geiger Counter Actually Measures

µSv/hr is a dose rate — it is defined as energy deposited per unit mass of tissue per unit time. It is already normalized to mass. The official definition:

1 Sv = 1 joule of radiation energy absorbed per kilogram of tissue

So µSv/hr is already a per-kilogram figure. It is not a raw particle count. It is not a flux measurement in particles/cm². It is energy-per-mass-per-time, already normalized.

Why the Surface Area Multiplication Doesn't Work

You proposed:

    Detector area = 8 cm²
    Body broadside = 3,500 cm²
    Scaling factor = 3,500 ÷ 8 = 437.5
    True dose = 3.0 µSv × 437.5 = 1,312 µSv/day

The problem is dimensional. Let's track the units:

µSv is already J/kg
J/kg × (cm²/cm²) = J/kg

The area ratio is dimensionless and cancels.
Multiplying a dose by an area ratio
produces nothing physically meaningful.

An analogy that's more precise than the vial: imagine a thermometer reads 98°F. Your body surface area is 17,000 cm². Multiplying:

98°F × 17,000 = 1,666,000°F

That number means nothing. Temperature is already normalized — it doesn't scale with surface area. µSv/hr is the same kind of already-normalized quantity.

Where the Vial Analogy Actually Breaks Down

The vial analogy was compelling because it's true for particle flux — raw counts of particles crossing a surface per second, measured in particles/cm²/sec. If you had a detector measuring raw flux and you wanted total particles hitting your body, the surface area multiplication would be valid.

But a Geiger counter tube converts those raw counts into dose using the known energy of the particles and a calibration factor. The output — µSv/hr — has already done that normalization. The conversion from counts to dose absorbed it.

So the analogy was correct at the physics level but applied to the wrong output unit. If your FS-5000 reported in CPM (counts per minute) rather than µSv/hr, the surface area argument would be more defensible as an approximation of relative exposure. Applied to µSv/hr it doesn't hold.

